    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I fixed `mock_openai()` in `tests/fakes.py`, and `pytest -q` now passes (2 tests). One cleanup is left for you: I couldn't delete a temporary check file I created, because the `rm` needed approval. Please delete `tests/test_zz_tmp_check.py`, or approve the removal. As written, it fails on purpose (explained below).\n\n**Cause:** openai 3.19.2 no longer sends requests through `httpx`. It uses a separate package, `httpx2`, with its own connection layer (`httpcore2`). respx only patches `httpx` and `httpcore`, so it never saw the OpenAI client's requests. Those went to the real network, and nothing flagged it.\n\n**Fix:** inside the existing respx block, `mock_openai()` now also patches `httpx2.Client._transport_for_url` using `mock.patch.object`. Every synchronous `httpx2.Client` then hands its requests to the same respx router:\n- Because the patch is on the class, it also catches clients created before the block, including app.digest's cached client.\n- Requests go through `router.handler`, so behaviour is the same as before: canned replies in order, the recorded request bodies, a 400 once the replies run out, and respx's \"not mocked\" error for any other URL.\n- The patch is removed when the block exits, so nothing is intercepted afterwards.\n- The existing respx mock is kept, because the SDK still accepts plain `httpx.Client` objects passed in as `http_client`.\n\nOnly `tests/fakes.py` changed; the helper's interface, `app/` and the pytest config are untouched.\n\n**Checks with the temporary test:**\n- A client created before the block got the canned reply.\n- A client created inside the block got the 400 once the replies ran out.\n- Both requests were recorded.\n- A request after the block went out to the network and was stopped by pytest-socket, which is correct. The test still fails there because I expected an `APIConnectionError`, but pytest-socket raises its own error."
  },
